        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Premature atrial complexes or beats (APC) that originate outside the sinoatrial node and disrupt the normal sinus rhythm for 1 or more beats.",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "ECG Features",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Heart rate usually normal; rhythm irregular",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "due to the premature P wave (called a P′",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "wave) that disrupts the normal P wave",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "rhythm (Figure 1).",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ectopic P′wave—",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "premature; configuration differs from that of",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "the sinus P waves and may be negative,",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "positive, biphasic, or superimposed on the",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "previous T wave.",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "QRS complex—",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "premature; configuration usually normal",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "(same as that of the sinus complexes). If the",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "P′wave occurs during the refractory period of",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "the atrioventricular (AV) node, ventricular",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "conduction does not occur (nonconducted",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "APCs), so no QRS complex follows the P′",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "wave. If there is partial recovery in the AV",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "node or intraventricular conduction systems,",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "the P′wave is conducted with a long P′–R",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "interval or with an abnormal QRS configura-",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "tion (aberrant conduction). The more",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "premature the complex, the more marked the",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "aberration.",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In the P–QRS relationship, the",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "P′–R interval is usually as long as, or longer",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "than, the sinus P′–R interval.",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "A noncom-",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "pensatory pause—when the R–R interval of",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "the two normal sinus complexes enclosing an",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "APC is less than the R–R intervals of three",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "consecutive sinus complexes—usually follows",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "an APC (Figure 2). The ectopic atrial impulse",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "discharges the sinus node and resets the cycle.",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "ECG Features"
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Mechanisms—an increase in automaticity of atrial myocardial fibers or a single reentrant",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "circuit.",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May be normal finding in aged APCs in a dog. P′represents the premature P wave. The premature QRS resembles the normal (sinus) QRS. The upright P′wave is superimposed on the T wave of the preceding complex. (Source: From Tilley LP. Essentials of Canine and Feline Electrocardiography, 3rd ed. Blackwell Publishing, 1992. Reprinted with permission of Wolters Kluwer.)",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "dogs; commonly seen in dogs with atrial enlargement secondary to chronic mitral valvular insufficiency; may also be observed",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "in dogs or cats with any atrial disease.",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May not cause hemodynamic problems; the clinical significance relates to their frequency, timing relative to other complexes, and the",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "underlying clinical problems.",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Can presage more serious rhythm disturbances (e.g., atrial fibrillation, atrial flutter, or atrial tachycardia).",
              "pdf_page": 221,
              "printed_page": 139,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
